WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:04.500
Thanos' adopted daughter is the highlight
of almost every Guardians of the Galaxy story

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:08.860
she appears in, but if you only know Gamora
from the Marvel Cinematic Universe, you're

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:10.099
missing out.

00:00:10.099 --> 00:00:12.429
She's much more than a world-class assassin.

00:00:12.429 --> 00:00:17.080
She's also a time traveler, a mother, a fashion
icon, and a lover... and she's wielded one

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:19.420
of the most powerful weapons ever made.

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:24.580
Gamora's comic backstory is full of twists
and turns that are crazy in all the best ways.

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:28.090
There's a reason Gamora is called "the most
dangerous woman in the universe."

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:33.850
Let's take a look at some of the story elements
you might not know about Marvel's green superheroine.

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:35.679
Unfortunate origin

00:00:35.679 --> 00:00:38.850
In the MCU, Gamora's origin is pretty straightforward.

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:43.010
Her planet was wiped out by Thanos, who rescued
her and raised her to be a warrior.

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:47.699
She eventually turned on Thanos, teamed up
with Star-Lord, Rocket, Drax, and Groot, and

00:00:47.699 --> 00:00:48.949
the rest is history.

00:00:48.949 --> 00:00:53.940
In the comics, Gamora first appeared in Strange
Tales #180, but we don't learn her history

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:55.789
until Warlock #10.

00:00:55.789 --> 00:01:01.100
As the story goes, Thanos correctly predicted
that the villainous Magus, a time-warped version

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:05.640
of hero Adam Warlock and leader of the tyrannical
Universal Church of Truth, threatened his

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.470
plans for galactic domination.

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:12.010
So, Thanos needed a weapon he could use to
stop Magus before he grew too powerful.

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:14.670
He found one, in the future.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:20.170
Years after Warlock #10 takes place, Magus'
soldiers killed Gamora's kinsfolk, the pacifist

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:22.770
Zen Whoberis as part of their crusade.

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:27.020
Thanos, however, managed to rescue Gamora
at the last minute, took her to the present,

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:29.950
and trained her to stop Magus once and for
all.

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:34.890
Her assassination attempt fails, and Gamora
flat-out perishes after turning on Thanos,

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:38.860
but she still helps Adam Warlock beat Magus
and the Church anyway.

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:40.530
Unseen action

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:44.470
Like her origin story, Gamora's earliest days
are pretty confusing.

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:48.970
After two appearances in Strange Tales, her
story moves into the Warlock standalone series.

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:54.320
Then, the struggle between Thanos, Adam, and
Gamora concludes in Avengers Annual #7 and

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:56.830
Marvel Two-in-One Annual #2.

00:01:56.830 --> 00:02:01.690
As a result of all of this ping-ponging between
different books, there's one big hole: while

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:06.170
Gamora eventually learns about Thanos' true
nature and betrays him, we never actually

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:07.600
see the whole story.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:12.769
In Warlock #15, Gamora is tasked by Thanos
to hunt down Adam Warlock, but is attacked

00:02:12.769 --> 00:02:16.120
en route by Drax… but then the series ends.

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:19.109
Next we see her, she's been left for dead
by Thanos himself.

00:02:19.109 --> 00:02:20.599
So, what happened?

00:02:20.599 --> 00:02:24.650
Seeing Gamora betray her surrogate father
should've been a real big moment, but it was

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:27.010
reduced to three flashback panels.

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:31.671
According to author Jim Starlin, Warlock was
ultimately cancelled due to poor sales, so

00:02:31.671 --> 00:02:36.040
he had limited pages to wrap the story up,
which meant supporting characters like Gamora

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:40.790
got the short end of the stick, leaving her
epic family drama as a footnote.

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:41.849
Nine lives

00:02:41.849 --> 00:02:46.290
Gamora might be good at taking lives, but
that doesn't mean she hasn't lost her own

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:47.290
a time or two.

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:51.849
When she was a kid, she snuck out against
Thanos' wishes on the planet Tartoonla #7

00:02:51.849 --> 00:02:55.409
and got attacked and slaughtered a by a roving
street gang.

00:02:55.409 --> 00:02:58.790
Thanos had to rebuild her from artificial
parts, and she lost her tear ducts in the

00:02:58.790 --> 00:02:59.790
deal.

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:04.379
We also see Gamora die in Avengers Annual
#7, and while her body perishes, her soul

00:03:04.379 --> 00:03:09.019
is saved by Adam Warlock, but her spirit,
along with Warlock's and Pip the Troll's,

00:03:09.019 --> 00:03:13.580
ended up safely ensconced inside the Soul
Gem, one of the Infinity Stones.

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:18.830
She returns to life in Infinity Gauntlet #1,
published about 14 years later, but there's

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:23.060
a catch: her soul is put into the corpse of
a woman named Bambi Long.

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:26.840
Even when Gamora is alive, she's still technically
not.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:31.349
When Thanos uses the Infinity Gauntlet to
end half of the life in the universe, Gamora

00:03:31.349 --> 00:03:34.940
bites it again, although Nebula resurrects
her and everyone else.

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:39.120
In other words, Gamora might be one of the
strongest heroes in the universe, but her

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:41.590
life tends to come and go.

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:42.590
Gem carrier

00:03:42.590 --> 00:03:47.450
So far, Marvel's movies have been all about
the Infinity Stones, six brightly colored

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:52.249
and infinitely powerful rocks that act as
the MacGuffins for the entire MCU.

00:03:52.249 --> 00:03:55.370
The Red Skull used one to fuel his superweapons.

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:59.790
Loki's scepter held the Mind Stone, which
later ended up bringing the Vision to life.

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:04.219
The Reality Stone pops up in Thor: The Dark
World, the Time Stone plays a crucial role

00:04:04.219 --> 00:04:08.639
in Doctor Strange, and the Power Stone is
the thing that drives the action in Guardians

00:04:08.639 --> 00:04:09.859
of the Galaxy.

00:04:09.859 --> 00:04:13.840
The Infinity Gems have comic book counterparts,
of course, although on the printed page they're

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:16.440
better known as the Soul or Infinity Gems.

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:21.160
They've been around since 1972, and they play
a big role in Infinity Gauntlet, which is

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:23.930
a foundation for Avengers: Infinity War.

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:26.740
It's not just Thanos that's wielded the gems,
however.

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:31.259
After Infinity Gauntlet, Adam Warlock took
control of the Infinity Gems, but higher powers

00:04:31.259 --> 00:04:35.340
than him decided that it was too much power
for one person to handle, and distributed

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:39.800
the gems among some of Marvel's cosmic heroes,
including Gamora.

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:44.060
As part of the superteam known as Infinity
Watch, Gamora was entrusted with guarding

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:45.229
the Time Gem.

00:04:45.229 --> 00:04:49.129
Now, Gamora didn't actually know how to use
the Time Gem, although she'd occasionally

00:04:49.129 --> 00:04:53.080
get prophetic visions thanks to the gem's
influence, and she eventually gave up her

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:54.680
duties and left the Watch.

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:59.240
The gems were stolen and Infinity Watch disbanded
shortly thereafter.

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:00.240
Mommy dearest

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:05.650
By now, you might've noticed that Adam Warlock
plays a big part in Gamora's pre-Guardians

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:06.650
life.

00:05:06.650 --> 00:05:10.860
Given the laws of comic book relationships,
that means that they'll inevitably hook up.

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:15.250
Warlock and Gamora didn't just become a couple,
however, they actually have a child together,

00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:16.819
albeit an adopted one.

00:05:16.819 --> 00:05:22.270
In Infinity Abyss, Jim Starlin's 2002 entry
in his long-running "Infinity Saga," five

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:26.990
clones of Thanos called the Thanosi set out
to kill Thanos and destroy the universe.

00:05:26.990 --> 00:05:30.169
Naturally, Warlock, Gamora, and their friends
try to stop them.

00:05:30.169 --> 00:05:35.870
The key to the Thanosi's scheme is an all-powerful
being called Atlez, the "anchor of reality"

00:05:35.870 --> 00:05:37.740
since the days of the Roman Empire.

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:41.900
When Atlez realizes his time is running out,
he realizes he needs to pass on his knowledge

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:45.770
to a human girl named Atleza, who was born
for this purpose.

00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:48.910
There's just one problem: Atleza is still
a little girl.

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:53.319
Gamora and her buddies end up defeating the
Thanosi, but Atleza, who's kinda overwhelmed

00:05:53.319 --> 00:05:55.970
with her newfound cosmic powers, needs training.

00:05:55.970 --> 00:06:00.439
As such, Gamora and Warlock retire to a pocket
dimension to teach her the ways of the universe

00:06:00.439 --> 00:06:02.580
while also serving as her foster parents.

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:07.120
It's one of the weirdest families imaginable,
but for Warlock and Gamora, weird is an everyday

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:08.120
thing.

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:11.229
With the two of them at her side, Atleza should
be just fine.

00:06:11.229 --> 00:06:12.229
Thanks for watching!

00:06:12.229 --> 00:06:14.560
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:16.729
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

